<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">evasion</ansi>

Every fighter, you, your companions and every enemy, has two combat
ratings that grow with level. Skill, not a pile of hit points, decides
who wins a fight between fighters of different levels.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Attack</ansi>    your skill at landing blows
  <ansi fg="yellow">Evasion</ansi>   your skill at not being hurt by them: dodging,
            parrying, or blocking

<ansi fg="command">status</ansi> shows both, and every level-up reports what they gained:

  <ansi fg="command">Attack 12 -> 13   Evasion 11 -> 12</ansi>

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">What each class gains a level</ansi>

  <ansi fg="yellow">Warrior, Samurai</ansi>   Attack 1     Evasion 1      (level 30: 30 / 30)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Rogue</ansi>              Attack 0.9   Evasion 1.1    (level 30: 27 / 33)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Ranger, Halberdier</ansi> Attack 1     Evasion 0.9    (level 30: 30 / 27)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Cleric, Wizard, Witch</ansi>  Attack 0.7   Evasion 0.75   (level 30: 21 / 22)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Shaman</ansi>             Attack 0.7   Evasion 0.75   (level 30: 21 / 22)
  <ansi fg="yellow">Enemies</ansi>            Attack {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.DefaultAttackRate" }}     Evasion {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.DefaultEvasionRate" }}

Fractions round down. A trained enemy captain or a clumsy brute may be a
few points above or below that. Casters are easier to hit than fighters:
their strength is their spells. Losing a level on death lowers both.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">How a gap changes a fight</ansi>

Your Attack against your target's Evasion gives a skill edge: each point
of difference is 1/{{ config "GamePlay.Combat.SkillEdgeSpan" }} of a full edge, so a {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.SkillEdgeSpan" }}-point lead is the most
there is. The edge is added to the stat edge each chance already uses
(<ansi fg="command">help stat-edge</ansi>), so a quick fighter is still harder to hit.

  <ansi fg="yellow">Chance</ansi>        <ansi fg="yellow">even</ansi>      <ansi fg="yellow">worst</ansi>   <ansi fg="yellow">best</ansi>
  To hit        {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ToHitEven" }}%       {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ToHitMin" }}%     {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ToHitMax" }}%
  Dodge         {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.DodgeChanceEven" }}%       {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.DodgeChanceMin" }}%      {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.DodgeChanceMax" }}%
  Parry         {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ParryChanceEven" }}%       {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ParryChanceMin" }}%      {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ParryChanceMax" }}%, plus the weapon's modifier
  Block         {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.BlockChanceEven" }}% + shield  {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.BlockChanceMin" }}%      {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.BlockChanceMax" }}%

For example, a level 18 warrior swinging at a level 10 warrior (an 8-point
lead, the most there is) hits 95% of the time and meets a 3% dodge. The
level 10 warrior swinging back hits only 10% of the time and meets a 40%
dodge.
A {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.ToHitMin" }}% floor keeps a lucky blow possible, so a pack of weaker foes is
still a threat. Critical hits stay with Smarts (<ansi fg="command">help smarts</ansi>).

A blow that lands is glancing, solid, or telling, and the same edge makes a
telling one likelier (<ansi fg="command">help attack</ansi>).

Spells use the same gap: a damage spell lands half to one and a half
times its listed damage, by the caster's Attack against the target's
Evasion (<ansi fg="command">help spells</ansi>).

Armor heavier than your training costs {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.UntrainedSkillLoss" }} Attack and {{ config "GamePlay.Combat.UntrainedSkillLoss" }} Evasion
(<ansi fg="command">help armor</ansi>). <ansi fg="command">consider</ansi> tells you in words how a foe's skill compares
with yours.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help attack</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help defense</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help shields</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help progression</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help combat</ansi>.
